schema: design.v1
name: persistent-memory-architecture-for-agents
version: 2.0.0
status: adopted

agent:
  skill: design
  instructions: |
    READ this file before generating or editing any UI in this repository —
    deck/ and web/ both consume it. FOLLOW tokens as
    normative — never hardcode a colour, radius, duration or type size that has a
    token. UPDATE this file in place when a real decision changes. VERIFY against
    constraints.never before finishing.

    The one rule that carries the whole system: DEPTH IS A SOLID DARKER EDGE,
    NEVER A BLUR. Every raised surface pairs a fill with its darker sibling and
    offsets it with `box-shadow: 0 <lip> 0 <sibling>`. Pressing translates the
    element down by exactly the lip and removes the shadow, so it lands flush.
    A soft blurred shadow anywhere in this deck is a bug.

overview: >
  A database-systems review of Notion's Lore, published as three editions from
  one source: a talk deck, a web paper, and a printed PDF. The visual language is
  Duolingo's, re-derived rather than copied — the rules are taken from
  design.duolingo.com and from Duolingo's shipped production CSS, then applied to
  material Duolingo has never rendered: entity-relationship diagrams, round-trip
  counts, validity intervals. Nothing here reproduces Duolingo's marks, its
  bespoke typefaces, or its characters.

surfaces:
  deck: >
    deck/, generated by tools/deck.mjs. Full-bleed Duolingo — the language at its
    loudest, because a slide is read at the back of a room.
  web: >
    web/, the paper's screen edition. The same tokens, the same lip, the same
    Nunito, applied to a document that is read for forty minutes rather than
    glanced at: the chrome carries the language and the reading column stays calm.
  print: >
    The PDF, rendered from the same page. It inherits NONE of this. Source Serif
    on a 130mm measure, booktabs rules, no colour and no rounding, because it is a
    review paper and not a screenshot of a website. The boundary is one appended
    `@media print` block at the foot of web/app/globals.css; the load-bearing line
    in it re-points `--font-sans` away from Nunito, which is what keeps a rounded
    display face out of forty-three pages of running heads.

intent:
  reference: >
    A Duolingo lesson screen that happens to be teaching relational algebra.
    Big rounded geometry, flat fills with no outlines, solid-lip depth, uppercase
    labels, and one bright accent per idea — carrying charts and schema diagrams
    that are read at the back of a lecture theatre.
  direction: >
    Playful geometry, serious numbers. The chrome is exuberant; every figure that
    carries evidence is drawn plainly, with a zero baseline and tabular numerals.
    The joke is that a system critique looks like a language lesson; the joke
    stops at the data.
  signature: >
    The lip. Buttons, cards, table rows, chart bars, the mascots and the slide
    counter all sit on a solid darker edge. Nothing else in the deck is allowed
    to be loud.
  treatment: editorial

voice:
  register: >
    Duolingo's four qualities, read off design.duolingo.com/writing/voice:
    expressive, playful, embracing, worldly. Brief, active, direct. Contractions
    are fine. Clever in quick doses, never smug and never sarcastic.
  casing:
    buttons: UPPERCASE, no punctuation ever inside a button
    headings: sentence case
    labels: UPPERCASE with 0.04em tracking
  numbers: >
    Numeric even below ten. Comma above 999 in prose; no comma inside a measured
    quantity rendered as a stat. Never hyphenate a compound like "3 req/s".
  punctuation: >
    Serial comma always. No semicolons. Avoid em dashes in UI copy — split the
    sentence. No punctuation in headlines except an exclamation mark.
  errors: >
    Support briefly, do not over-apologise. "Not quite. The store can answer it —
    it just costs 1 213 requests." Never "Incorrect."
  forbidden:
    - Duo, the owl, or any Duolingo character name
    - "Powered by", "Leverage", "Seamless", "Unlock"
    - exclamation marks on any slide that reports a defect

tokens:
  color:
    # Brand-role colours. Every fill names its darker sibling, because the lip
    # is the whole visual language. Names follow Duolingo's light-to-dark animal
    # families so the pairing is legible in the token names themselves.
    canvas: "#FFFFFF"
    canvas-sunk: "#F7F7F7"
    ink: "#3C3C3C"
    # Duolingo's own Hare (#777) is 4.48:1 on white and 4.01:1 on the inset grey
    # these labels actually sit on. A muted grey has to clear the floor against
    # the DARKEST surface it is set on, not against the canvas — checking it
    # against white was 57 real contrast failures in the web edition.
    ink-muted: "#6B6B6B"        # 5.33:1 on canvas, 4.63:1 on #EFEFEF
    ink-faint: "#636363"        # 6.02:1 on canvas, 5.23:1 on #EFEFEF
    ink-disabled: "#AFAFAF"     # decoration and rules only, never text
    rule: "#E5E5E5"

    # Each family is a quad, and the fourth member is the one Duolingo does not
    # ship. `-lip` is an EDGE colour and may never carry text: feather green
    # under white measures about 2:1, and Duolingo's own primary buttons fail
    # WCAG AA on exactly that pair. `-ink` is the darkened sibling that clears
    # 4.5:1 on both the canvas and the tint. Every piece of text uses `-ink`.
    relational: "#58CC02"
    relational-lip: "#58A700"
    relational-tint: "#D7FFB8"
    relational-ink: "#37700A"   # 6.03:1 on canvas, 5.42:1 on tint
    # A fourth job the deck never needed: a fill that HOLDS white text. Feather
    # green under white is 2.09:1, which fails even the 3:1 large-text floor, so
    # a primary button cannot be the brand green. This is the darkest green that
    # still reads as the family and clears AA with white on it (4.91:1).
    relational-strong: "#3C8000"
    relational-strong-lip: "#2F6600"

    substrate: "#1CB0F6"
    substrate-lip: "#1899D6"
    substrate-tint: "#DDF4FF"
    substrate-ink: "#0A6187"    # 6.83:1 on canvas, 6.01:1 on tint

    defect: "#FF4B4B"
    defect-lip: "#EA2B2B"
    defect-tint: "#FFDFE0"
    defect-ink: "#B81C1C"       # 6.52:1 on canvas, 5.24:1 on tint

    cost: "#FFC800"
    cost-lip: "#E7A601"
    cost-tint: "#FFF5D3"
    cost-ink: "#7A5600"         # 6.65:1 on canvas, 6.10:1 on tint

    temporal: "#CE82FF"
    temporal-lip: "#9069CD"
    temporal-tint: "#F3E4FF"
    temporal-ink: "#5D3F90"     # 8.14:1 on canvas, 6.73:1 on tint

    deep: "#2B70C9"
    deep-lip: "#1453A3"

  typography:
    # Feather Bold and duolingo-sans are both unlicensable. Nunito is Duolingo's
    # own designated substitute, named on design.duolingo.com/identity/typography,
    # and it is the only legally clean route to this feel.
    family-display: '"Nunito", "Baloo 2", ui-rounded, system-ui, sans-serif'
    family-ui: '"Nunito", ui-rounded, system-ui, sans-serif'
    family-mono: '"JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, monospace'
    weight-display: "900"
    weight-strong: "800"
    weight-body: "600"
    weight-label: "800"
    size-slide-title: "clamp(2.6rem, 5.2vw, 4.4rem)"
    size-heading: "clamp(1.6rem, 2.6vw, 2.2rem)"
    size-body: "clamp(1.05rem, 1.5vw, 1.35rem)"
    size-caption: "0.95rem"
    size-label: "0.82rem"
    size-stat: "clamp(3rem, 7vw, 6rem)"
    leading-display: "1.05"
    leading-body: "1.5"
    tracking-display: "-0.02em"
    tracking-label: "0.04em"

  radius:
    sm: "8px"
    md: "12px"
    lg: "16px"
    xl: "24px"
    pill: "9999px"

  space:
    "1": "4px"
    "2": "8px"
    "3": "12px"
    "4": "16px"
    "5": "20px"
    "6": "24px"
    "8": "32px"
    "10": "40px"
    "12": "48px"
    "16": "64px"
    "24": "96px"

  border:
    stroke: "2px"
    lip-sm: "2px"
    lip-md: "4px"
    lip-lg: "6px"

  motion:
    ease-pop: "cubic-bezier(.35, 1.8, .35, .83)"
    ease-settle: "cubic-bezier(.22, 1, .36, 1)"
    dur-press: "100ms"
    dur-fast: "200ms"
    dur-base: "300ms"
    dur-slow: "400ms"

  elevation:
    # There is no blur in this system. Elevation is the lip.
    raised: "0 4px 0 var(--lip-color)"
    resting: "0 2px 0 var(--lip-color)"
    flush: "none"

policy:
  hierarchy: [typography, space, contrast, color]
  color:
    accent_cycle: [relational, substrate, cost, temporal, defect]
    rule: >
      One accent family per slide. A second family appears only when the slide is
      literally comparing two things, and then it is always relational vs
      substrate. Defect red never decorates; it only marks something that is
      actually broken.
  if_missing: nearest
  a11y: >
    WCAG 2.2 AA for every text-on-fill pair. The -ink tokens are the whole
    answer: each clears 5.2:1 against both the canvas and its own tint, verified
    arithmetically rather than by eye. Every animation has a prefers-reduced-motion path that keeps the state
    change and drops the movement. The deck is keyboard-navigable and every slide
    is reachable without a pointer.

constraints:
  always:
    - Pair every fill with its -lip sibling and offset the shadow by a lip token.
    - Press states translate down by exactly the lip and set the shadow to none.
    - Illustration is flat fill with zero strokes; UI chrome is a 2px stroke.
    - Build illustration from three primitives only — rounded rect, circle, rounded triangle.
    - Chart bars start at zero and use tabular numerals.
    - Uppercase labels carry 0.04em tracking.
    - Text takes an -ink token. -lip tokens are edges and never carry text.
  never:
    - A blurred box-shadow anywhere.
    - A pointed corner on an illustrated shape.
    - Grey inside an illustration — use the pale tints instead.
    - An outline on an illustrated shape.
    - Duolingo's marks, characters, or bespoke typefaces.
    - Punctuation inside a button label.
    - An oval drop shadow under a character — shadows are pills.
    - White text on a bright fill. Duolingo does it; it fails AA; we do not.
    - A truncated chart axis.

components:
  button:
    when: navigation between slides, the presenter's own controls
    notes: >
      Deliberately the quiet variant, not the loud one. A bright fill under white
      text is the one pair this language gets wrong, and deck chrome is not worth
      failing a contrast check for. The lip mechanism is identical; only the fill
      and the text colour change.
    tokens:
      background: color.canvas
      lip: color.rule
      textColor: color.ink
      rounded: radius.md
      height: "44px"
      padding: "0 var(--space-4)"
      typography: "weight-label / size-label / tracking-label / uppercase"
    variants:
      loud: { background: color.relational, lip: color.relational-lip, textColor: color.ink, notes: "ink, never white" }
      quiet: { background: transparent, lip: none, textColor: color.ink-muted }
  card:
    when: a claim, a finding, a table row, a schema box
    tokens:
      background: color.canvas
      border: "var(--stroke) solid var(--rule)"
      lip: color.rule
      rounded: radius.md
      padding: "var(--space-5)"
    variants:
      accented: { border: "var(--stroke) solid var(--accent)", lip: accent-lip, background: accent-tint }
  stat:
    when: a single measured number that carries a slide
    tokens:
      typography: "weight-display / size-stat / leading-display / tabular-nums"
      textColor: accent
  meter:
    when: comparing a cost across arms
    tokens:
      track: color.rule
      fill: accent
      rounded: radius.pill
      height: "16px"
    notes: >
      Carries the gloss pill Duolingo's progress bar uses — a white pill at 20%
      opacity in the top third of the fill. It is what makes the bar read as
      inflated rather than flat.
  bubble:
    when: a quotation from Lore's own source
    tokens:
      background: color.canvas
      border: "var(--stroke) solid var(--rule)"
      rounded: radius.lg
    notes: >
      The tail is a 45-degree rotated rounded square inheriting the bubble's
      border, not a CSS border triangle. That is why the tip is rounded and the
      outline stays continuous.

patterns:
  slide:
    treatment: editorial
    recipe: >
      One idea per slide. An uppercase eyebrow label in the accent, a sentence-case
      title in display weight, then exactly one of: a stat, a chart, a diagram, or
      at most three cards. Never two of those together.
  evidence:
    treatment: utilitarian
    recipe: >
      Slides that carry measured numbers drop the exuberance: white ground, ink
      text, one accent, zero baseline, the source of the number stated under the
      figure in caption size.

decisions:
  accent:
    - if: the slide is about Notion, the Data API, or the vault as shipped
      then: substrate (blue)
    - if: the slide is about SQL, constraints, or the reimplementation
      then: relational (green)
    - if: the slide reports a round-trip count, a byte count, or a rate limit
      then: cost (yellow)
    - if: the slide is about validity intervals or bitemporality
      then: temporal (purple)
    - if: the slide reports something that is actually broken
      then: defect (red)
  motion:
    - if: an element enters the viewport
      then: translateY with ease-pop over dur-fast
    - if: a layout or scroll position changes
      then: ease-settle over dur-slow
    - if: the user presses something
      then: translateY by the lip over dur-press, no easing curve needed
    - if: prefers-reduced-motion is set
      then: keep opacity, drop every transform

rationale:
  colors: >
    Duolingo's palette is not a set of colours, it is a set of light-to-dark
    families. That is why the illustration ramp is named for animals in pairs and
    why every button ships a fill and a lip. Adopting the families rather than the
    hexes is what makes an original system feel like this one.
  elevation: >
    The single most transferable idea in Duolingo's language: elevation is a solid
    darker edge, never a soft blur. It reads at any distance, survives projection,
    and costs nothing to render.
  typography: >
    Body weight is 600, not 400. Rounded geometric faces go weedy at 400, which is
    why Duolingo ships body text at weight 500 and 20px. A lecture theatre wants
    more, not less.
  shapes: >
    Three primitives, rounded everything, no outlines, about fifteen shapes per
    illustration. Six is too abstract and thirty is noise; Duolingo's own
    guidance gives those counts.

sources:
  - url: https://design.duolingo.com/identity/color
    took: brand palette, neutral ramp, colour hierarchy
  - url: https://design.duolingo.com/identity/typography
    took: type roles, leading and tracking rules, Nunito as the sanctioned substitute
  - url: https://design.duolingo.com/illustration/shape-language
    took: three primitives, shape counts, flat perspective, pill shadows, no grey
  - url: https://design.duolingo.com/illustration/characters
    took: character anatomy rules, geometric eyes, asymmetric mouths
  - url: https://design.duolingo.com/writing/voice
    took: the four voice qualities and their is/is-not lists
  - url: https://design.duolingo.com/writing/style
    took: uppercase buttons, no punctuation in buttons, numeral rules, serial comma
  - note: >
      Duolingo's shipped production CSS, read directly. The brand site publishes
      no tokens, components, motion or spacing routes; every component and motion
      value here was measured from the live app and is marked as such.
    took: lip mechanism, radii census, the two easing curves, duration census

omitted:
  - integrations
  - exports
  - assets

locked:
  - tokens.color.relational
  - tokens.color.substrate
  - constraints.never
